menu "SD stream server"

choice APP_BOARD
    prompt "Which board is this firmware for"
    default APP_BOARD_DEVKIT
    help
        The board decides the display. The development board has none, so the interface
        is rendered into a framebuffer this firmware owns (and can be dumped over the
        console to look at). The Sunton 8048S050C has an 800x480 RGB panel whose two
        framebuffers live in PSRAM and are scanned out by the RGB peripheral.

        Per-board settings that are not a Kconfig choice - PSRAM and flash speed above
        all - live in a separate defaults file: see sdkconfig.defaults.sunton and the
        README's board section.

config APP_BOARD_DEVKIT
    bool "ESP32-S3 development board (no panel)"

config APP_BOARD_SUNTON_8048S050C
    bool "Sunton 8048S050C (800x480 RGB panel, GT911 touch)"

endchoice

config APP_UI_SERIAL_DUMP
    bool "Dump the rendered screen over the serial console once at startup"
    default n
    help
        With no panel attached this is how the interface can be looked at: the
        framebuffer is sent as base64 rows ("UIROW <n> <data>") a few seconds after
        boot, and tools/decode_screen_dump.py turns a captured log into a PNG. Off by
        default: it is a diagnostic, and it slows the boot it is enabled on.

        See docs/research/radio-player-memory-and-lvgl.md.

config APP_UI_DUMP_STRIDE
    int "Sample every Nth pixel when dumping the screen"
    depends on APP_UI_SERIAL_DUMP
    default 2
    range 1 8
    help
        The console runs at 115200 baud, so a full 800x480 dump takes minutes. Every
        second pixel in both directions makes it 400x240 and about twenty seconds,
        which is enough to check a layout and quick enough to iterate on.

endmenu
